    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/341146": {
      "title": "Dean of School of Health Sciences",
      "description": "Dean of School of Health Sciences You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Dean of School of Health Sciences Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description THE OPPORTUNITY Columbia College (the College), a private liberal arts college with a legacy of transformation and impact, seeks a seasoned leader to serve as its Dean of the School of Health Sciences. This is a defining opportunity for a bold leader to join a college on the rise, reimagining liberal arts education through inquiry-based learning, deep community engagement, and a steadfast commitment to social mobility. The Dean will be an architect of possibility, helping to articulate and advance the School of Health Science’s mission in ways that expand opportunity and empower students to shape meaningful futures. The Dean will mold the academic strategy, steward resources, and cultivate a culture of excellence in teaching, learning, research, and scholarship for the School. The Dean will collaborate closely across campus to strengthen academic planning, support innovation, and ensure that the School of Health Science’s programs remain responsive to student and societal needs. The Dean will be a key driver of this work, leading efforts to amplify the School of Health Science’s academic identity, strengthen community partnerships, and shape programs that allow students to thrive as engaged citizens and professionals. ABOUT COLUMBIA COLLEGE Columbia College is a private liberal arts institution committed to preparing students for lives of purpose, responsibility, and impact. Since its founding, the College has cultivated a learning environment where academic rigor is paired with a deep investment in personal growth, ethical leadership, and community engagement. Leadership at the College is cultivated through action, reflection, and connection, not defined by titles or hierarchy. Students are encouraged to engage deeply with their communities, take initiative, and approach challenges with resilience. Whether in the classroom, through service, or in professional settings, they learn to lead with empathy and integrity. The College offers undergraduate, graduate, and online programs that blend a liberal arts foundation with career-focused preparation. Small class sizes and close faculty mentorship foster a culture of support. This is a moment of forward momentum for the College. With a new Provost and Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs as well as the launch of a strategic plan that guides progress toward the end of the decade, the College is investing in academic areas that align with workforce needs and directly serve the needs of its surrounding communities. Most graduates remain in South Carolina, and the institution takes seriously its role in preparing students to contribute meaningfully to the region’s social and economic vitality. History Founded in 1854 and rooted in the United Methodist Church, Columbia College has a rich legacy as one of the oldest institutions of higher learning in South Carolina. Originally established as Columbia Female College, the institution opened its doors to students in 1859 with a mission to provide a rigorous liberal arts education for women. Over the decades, Columbia College has evolved in response to societal shifts, education needs, and student demographics, becoming fully coeducational in 2020 and welcoming its first coed residential class in 2021. Throughout its history, Columbia College has demonstrated resilience and adaptability. The campus has endured and rebuilt after fires, including a major one in 1964 that destroyed Old Main, a beloved campus landmark. The surviving columns of Old Main remain a powerful symbol for the College and have since been incorporated into the logo. Today, the College offers a personalized liberal arts education that is open and inclusive to all. The College is committed to cultivating critical thinking, effective communication, lifelong learning, and a strong sense of personal responsibility. Strategic Vision In 2025, Columbia College launched On the Rise: A Plan for Growth and Impact , a strategic framework designed to guide institutional transformation through the end of the decade. The plan reflects the College’s vision for the future: to be a model of intentional liberal arts education, a champion of inquiry-based and experiential learning, and a catalyst for community transformation. The College’s academic approach redefines what it means to be a liberal arts institution. It moves beyond knowledge acquisition to foster critical thinking, creative problem-solving, and collaborative inquiry. Graduates will be prepared for their first job and the challenges and opportunities of a future that can’t yet be fully imagined. Columbia College envisions a campus deeply embedded in its surrounding community, working in partnership to dismantle the barriers that undermine health and opportunity. The School of Health Sciences will have profound and reciprocal community engagement, and faculty, staff, and students will be actively engaged in addressing the social determinants of health in one of the most medically vulnerable zip codes in the country. Academic Schools The College offers more than 50 undergraduate majors, minors, and certificates, as well as graduate and online programs that support a wide range of academic and career goals. Small class sizes, close faculty mentorship, and a commitment to student success define the academic experience at Columbia College. Academic offerings are organized across four schools: • The School of Arts and Sciences provides a strong liberal arts foundation through programs in the humanities, social sciences, natural sciences, and the arts. • The School of Business and Leadership prepares students for careers in business, management, and organizational leadership in a rapidly evolving global economy. • The School of Education has a legacy of excellence in teacher preparation and offers degrees in early childhood, elementary, middle-level, and special education, as well as educational studies and leadership. Students benefit from strong partnerships with local schools and a focus on student-centered teaching practices. • The School of Health Sciences provides students with foundational knowledge in health and wellness while developing the skills needed for graduate study or entry into health-related professions. All students benefit from a robust mentoring model that includes an academic advisor, a staff advisor, and a peer mentor. This integrated support system helps students navigate academic planning, personal development, and the transition to campus life. As part of the College’s commitment to student success, all students take a first-year seminar course designed to help them build a strong foundation for college. Columbia College also offers the Dr. John Zubizarreta Honors Program , which provides an enriched academic experience for highly motivated students. Honors students participate in small, discussion-based seminars focused on critical thinking, creativity, and self-directed learning. The program includes experiential opportunities, global study experiences, and access to national honors organizations and fellowships. ROLE OF THE DEAN, SCHOOL OF HEALTH SCIENCES The Dean reports to the Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs and Provost. Alongside the other School Deans as well as the Dean of Library, Learning, and Student Success and the Dean of Online and Digital Learning, the Dean of the School of Health Sciences will provide overall academic leadership, administrative support, and coordination for academic affairs across the College. The Deans lead the College’s academic mission through transparent communication, adept change management, and collaborative decision-making. Further, the Deans foster a structure that supports distributed leadership, enabling each School to guide its programs, develop its faculty, and contribute meaningfully to the College’s priorities. In addition to being an academic leader, the Dean cultivates and inspires leadership at all levels of the College. KEY OPPORTUNITIES The Dean will join the College at a significant moment of transition and opportunity. To succeed, the Dean must be a visionary leader with proven experience in leading positive organizational and cultural change. The Dean will provide collaborative, strategic, and operational leadership for programs in both the natural sciences and allied health disciplines.  As a key member of the College’s academic leadership team, the Dean will guide growth in undergraduate programs and development of graduate and professional programs that respond to regional workforce needs, improve health outcomes for the larger community, and advance the priorities of the College’s strategic plan. Identifying and obtaining funding and resources to drive this growth will be key to this position. The Dean will help strengthen the School’s established programs in chemistry, biology, environmental health, public health, exercise science, and speech-language pathology while leading the evaluation and development of new academic programs. The Dean will connect academic quality, enrollment strategy, workforce alignment, experiential learning, research, community engagement and resource development into a coherent vision for the School. The successful candidate will be a relationship-centered leader who can build productive partnerships across and throughout the health and science communities. Primary responsibilities include supervising assistant and associate deans and program chairs; providing strategic direction/managing the School’s budget and funding/resources; supporting faculty and program development; overseeing assessment, accreditation, and continuous improvement; collaborating with Enrollment, Marketing, Advancement, Facilities, and other College units; and expanding clinical, research, community, and workforce partnerships. Qualifications PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES of the DEAN Oversees School-wide curriculum programs; Hires and supervises School faculty and staff; Manages and proposes school budget in collaboration with School’ two assistant deans; Serves as a liaison with administration and other College Schools; Works with admissions on recruitment and retention of quality students; Along with the Provost and assistant deans, when available, actively represents the School and College externally, especially in the health sciences professional community; Collaborates continuously with Development Office to identify and apply for grants and other external funding sources; Collaborates with Development Office to recruit and maintain active advisory boards for all programs. QUALIFICATIONS AND CHARACTERISTICS of the DEAN The successful candidate will possess the following required qualifications and characteristics: Terminal degree in natural, physical, clinical, allied health, public health, or closely related field; Record of teaching, scholarship and service sufficient for appointment at the professor level at Columbia College; Demonstrated ability to lead collaboratively across multiple disciplines and to work effectively with faculty, students, administrators, and external partners; Experience with academic planning, program assessment, faculty development, and resource management; Strong communication, organizational, and relationship-building skills; Leadership experience at the dean, associate dean, assistant dean, department chair, or comparable level; Personal and professional integrity; Ability to make complex, timely, and principled decisions; An entrepreneurial spirit to realize the ambitions of the School; A participatory and collaborative management style that empowers and develops their team. The successful candidate will possess the following preferred qualifications: At least three years of progressive administrative or leadership experience; Demonstrated success in budget management, enrollment planning, program growth, and development of sustainable academic programs; Experience with specialized accreditation, professional licensure, clinical education, or regulated health programs; Professional experience or sustained partnerships in health, science, public health, clinical, or allied health settings outside higher education; Record of developing community, clinical, workforce, research, or philanthropic partnerships; Success securing grants, gifts, appropriations, or other external resources; Experience supporting laboratory and clinical facilities, scientific instrumentation, or research infrastructure. COLUMBIA, SOUTH CAROLINA Columbia is the capital of South Carolina with a growing metropolitan population of over 800,000. The city is a central hub for government, healthcare, and commerce, and is home to more than 40,000 college students, contributing to a vibrant academic and research community. The city’s central location in the state's geographic center provides convenient access to the Atlantic coast and the Blue Ridge Mountains. Columbia’s downtown features over 70 restaurants and shops, with ongoing investment in infrastructure, housing, and public spaces. The Main Street District , Five Points , the Vista , and The Bull Street District have become key destinations for dining, arts, and community events, including the weekly Soda City Market , where vendors must make or grow their own products. The city supports a range of cultural institutions, including the Columbia Museum of Art , the South Carolina State Museum , and the Nickelodeon Theatre , South Carolina’s only nonprofit art house cinema. Columbia is also home to the Columbia Fireflies , the city’s minor league baseball team. Outdoor amenities are easily accessible, including the Three Rivers Greenway , which connects parks and neighborhoods along the Congaree, Saluda, and Broad Rivers. Just outside the city, Congaree National Park offers hiking and kayaking opportunities in one of the country's largest old-growth bottomland hardwood forests. APPLICATIONS, INQUIRIES, AND NOMINATIONS Inquiries may be directed to Executive Vice President and Provost, Dr. Gillian Stewart-Wells at gstewartwells@columbiasc.edu Please go to https://www.columbiasc.edu/about/employment to submit an application which should include a CV, a letter of intent, and transcripts for all graduate level work (unofficial transcripts may be submitted for the application process). Should you advance in the interview process, official transcripts as well as the names and contact information for references may be requested. Columbia College welcomes applications from candidates with disabilities. If you have any accommodation or access needs, we are happy to provide reasonable accommodations. Columbia College, an affirmative action, equal opportunity employer, is seeking candidates who will enhance its faculty, staff, and administration. Columbia College does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, ancestry, national origin, gender, identity sexual orientation, marital status, religion, age, disability, national origin, results of genetic testing or service in the military. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/339875": {
      "title": "Manager of Accessibility Services",
      "description": "Manager of Accessibility Services You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Manager of Accessibility Services Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description JOB SUMMARY The primary role of the Manager of Accessibility Services is to support the needs of students with disabilities by ensuring equal access to educational programs, services, and facilities at Columbia College. The Manager of Accessibility Services manages the approval process for accommodation and works with faculty and staff to improve accessibility services. The Manager of Accessibility Services ensures that Columbia College maintains compliance with Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act and the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC) accreditation. The Manager of Accessibility Services is responsible for conducting needs assessments, reporting on program effectiveness and providing assessments for all accreditations. The Manager of Accessibility Services also works closely with the leadership of Student Success, Faculty and Staff to ensure all students are provided with academic and professional development resources that foster successful behaviors and habits, while also increasing access to resources at Columbia College and in the community. Student Success supports the Columbia College mission by providing services and resources that empower all Columbia College students to explore and identify their academic and professional passions while acquiring the skills, experiences and connections needed to thrive academically and graduate prepared for their postgraduation goals. The department comprises three functional areas: Accessibility Services, Academic Advising, and Career Services. This position plays an important role in Columbia College’s retention efforts, and focuses on creating an academically, socially, and physically accessible environment for all students by removing barriers to learning. The Manager of Accessibility Services reports to the Dean of Library, Learning, & Student Success. The candidate is expected to have flexibility scheduling to meet the needs of the College. Columbia College is a liberal arts college that is committed to creating a learning community that values diversity and inclusion. We seek candidates who will be innovative in teaching, leadership, and research while building collaborative relationships in the college and profession. Columbia College offers a competitive benefits package. Qualifications ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: The essential functions of this position include, but are not limited to: Serve as the campus’s ADA Manager: In compliance with Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, Columbia College recognizes a student with a disability as anyone who has a physical or mental impairment that substantially limits one or more major life activities. Manage the accommodation process: Reviewing and interpreting documentation that substantiates disabilities. Conducting intake interviews. Determining eligibility for services and appropriate accommodation for students with faculty for classroom modifications. Mange the processing of accommodation letters for new applicants and returning students each semester. Manage the provision of services that may include, but are not limited to: Academic accommodation. Accessible housing. Interpreter and reader services. Specialized testing accommodations. Digital and audio book needs. Faculty consultations. Orientation to campus. Use of assistive devices. Resource information & referrals. Professional Development for faculty, staff, and students. Assessment Management Conducting needs assessment. Tracking overall success of campus’s ADA program. Keeping current with all the latest regulations and policies. Contributing to accreditation reports. Goals for this position include, but are not limited to: Creating equal opportunities for students with disabilities to learn and participate. Providing training and educational opportunities for the Columbia College community on disability issues, ensuring the community is knowledgeable and following best practices on issues of accessibility. Advocating for students and others in the learning community Serving as a campus resource, and subject matter expert, on accessibility services and accommodations for students, faculty, and staff Encouraging all students with disabilities to self-advocate, participate in leadership opportunities and develop the total self . Increasing the visibility and awareness of disability services and enhancing the quality, effectiveness, and efficiency of accessibility services and accommodations on campus. Administrative goals for this position include, but are not limited to: Manage resources to ensure academic access and retention of students with disabilities. Serve as a liaison with faculty and various campus offices in providing community resources for students. Manages the testing center. Document, evaluate, & implement accommodations for short-term illnesses and injuries. Manage and maintain medical documentation for students with accommodations. Manage and maintain the database systems for the Office of Accessibility including a data dashboard, and tables for housing and ESA accommodations solely for Resident Life. Manage and generate Jenzabar, Navigate and Slate reports to help identify at risk students. Manage the SC Assistive Technology Program that includes testing equipment that would benefit students. Manage the hardship accommodations process, including but not limited to the review and response to hardship notifications and sending of hardship notifications letters. Collaborate with the Title IX coordinator on accessibility, accommodation, and hardship cases, when appropriate. Communicate all pertinent information to any necessary party (faculty, staff, student, committee, facilities, etc.) regarding accessibility and accommodations issues/outcomes/prevention. Administrative & Operational Leadership Supervises, trains, develops, and evaluates designated faculty and staff as assigned by the Dean. Manages assigned budget areas and recommends resource allocation. Leads special projects and strategic initiatives assigned by the Dean and/or AD. Represents the school in internal committees and external meetings when appropriate. Management retains the discretion to add or change the duties of the position at any time. MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS TO PERFORM WORK: Master’s degree in disability services, special education, social work, human services, or counseling preferred; degree in a closely related field with relevant work experience required. At least one year of related professional experience working with students with disabilities; 3-5 years of experience working with students with disabilities in a higher education setting preferred. Deep knowledge of and experience with ADA compliance and knowledge of disability laws, including knowledge of the Rehabilitation Act (1973), the Americans with Disabilities Act (1990), and the ADA Amendments Act (2008), and associated case law as they relate to higher education. Ability to communicate and work collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and community. Resourceful in researching and implementing best practices in disability services, including new approaches or technologies that may be applicable to supporting our students. Strong written and oral communication skills. Strong attention to detail and follow through. Proficiency in Microsoft Office. Experience with EAB Navigate is a plus. Experience working with learners of all ages and backgrounds. Experience as a manager with programmatic oversight of accessibility services and accommodations preferred. Must be able to work some evening hours and occasional weekends. Preferred: Disability Services Provider (DSP) through AHEAD Preferred: Digital accessibility basics: WCAG 2.1 standards, web accessibility evaluation tools KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Proficient in technology, such as, deep knowledge Web Content Accessibility Guidelines and assistive technology. Proficient in understanding tools to automate testing. Communication & Advocacy Skills. Time Management Skills. Leadership and Team building skills. Listening and Critical thinking skills. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Requires sedentary work that involves walking, standing, lifting, carrying, pushing and pulling some of the time and involves exerting up to 10 pounds of force on a recurring basis or routine keyboard operations. WORKING CONDITIONS: Work environment risks exposure to no known environmental hazards. Applications To apply, a candidate must submit the following materials through the online submission portal (all in one pdf file except for the cover letter, which may be uploaded as a separate document): A cover letter describing teaching interests/experiences An up-to-date curriculum vitae A teaching statement that includes teaching philosophy and methodology A statement regarding research and other scholarly endeavors Names and contact information for three references, and Copies of graduate transcripts. Unofficial copies of transcripts will suffice for the initial application stage. Review of completed applications will begin September 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to Dean of Library, Learning, & Student Success, Dr. Monika Rhue, at mrhue@columbiasc.edu. About Columbia College Columbia College was founded in 1854 as Columbia Female College by the Methodist Conference of South Carolina. Today, Columbia College offers a personalized liberal arts education that is open and inclusive to all. The College provides educational opportunities that develop students’ capacity for critical thought and expression, lifelong learning, and acceptance of personal responsibility. Columbia College empowers students to change the world with Courage for Innovation, Commitment to Social Justice, Confidence through Service, and Competence in Leadership. In furtherance of its mission, the College is responsive to the needs of students, the communities to which it belongs, and the greater global society. Columbia College encompasses the historic undergraduate day college as well as evening, online, and graduate programs. Total enrollment is approximately 1850. The College is noted for faculty who foster academic excellence through collaborative teaching and learning; a vital learning community in which character, ethics, and values are cultivated; the study of leadership enhanced by opportunities for practical application; programs that capitalize on advances in technology; and learning environments that extend beyond the classroom. EOE M/F/D/V Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/330416": {
      "title": "Student Success Librarian",
      "description": "Student Success Librarian You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Student Success Librarian Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description JOB SUMMARY The Division of Integrated Learning Services at Columbia College invites applications for the Student Success Librarian position beginning September 2026 or earliest available date . The Student Success Librarian will advance the Division of Integrated Learning Services (ILS) by developing strategic outreach and academic engagement to support the library, career services, accessibility services, and the academic advising team, while promoting partnerships across Columbia College. The Student Success Librarian will provide support for ILS by working collaboratively in assessing, planning, and promoting all student success services to undergraduate, graduate, and online students at Columbia College. The Student Success Librarian will provide support services in the library by managing the electronic resources, providing instruction on electronic resources and other materials to increase students’ and faculty knowledge on services provided by the ILS division. The ideal candidate will be able to multi-task, have an interest in working with students, and feel comfortable building partnerships across Columbia College. The position reports to the Dean of Library, Learning, & Student Success while working collaboratively with other members of the ILS and Learning Commons faculty and staff. Participation in evening and weekend shifts is required, as needed. This position is a twelve-month full-time faculty appointment. The candidate is expected to have flexibility scheduling to meet the needs of the College. Faculty members are expected to participate in faculty governance and academic program activities, and to engage in service as well as scholarship and professional development. Columbia College is a liberal arts college that is committed to creating a learning community that values diversity and inclusion. We seek candidates who will be innovative in teaching, leadership, and research while building collaborative relationships in the college and profession. Columbia College offers a competitive benefits package. Qualifications ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Library Electronic Services Management (45%) Manage the Library's electronic and print collections in collaboration with librarians and faculty to ensure alignment with Columbia College’s academic programs and support services. Manage the purchasing and renewal process for electronic resources including collection development of print and electronic books. Manage the cataloging of library materials in collaboration with ILS Coordinator and the Library Assistant. Provide instruction and consultation to undergraduate, graduate, and online students, faculty, and staff on the use of electronic resources and library materials. Collaborate with the Educational Technology Librarian and Columbia College Instructional Designer in developing learning materials for using electronic resources, library materials, and other student support services materials for in-person and virtual learners. Provide in-person and virtual reference services to students, faculty, and staff. Manage all aspects of the Partnership Among South Carolina Academic Libraries (PASCAL) shared library services platform (currently Alma/Primo VE). Maintain strong working relationships with PASCAL staff and colleagues in the consortium. Monitor information from PASCAL, participate in webinars and other educational activities, and share with library faculty/staff of important information. Maintains all assessments for statistical reporting, such as ACRL, and Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC) with particular attention to assessing outcomes for undergraduate and graduate students understanding of how to effectively search for reliable sources. Apply data, assessment, and user feedback to evaluate effectiveness of library collections for both print and electronic resources, services, and programs. Serve as the contact for troubleshooting and reporting issues related to managing authentication and linkage with discovery tools, such as Primo VE and Open Athens. Develop policies and procedures relevant to electronic resources use; maintain current knowledge of licensing agreements. Collaborate with the library staff and student success in designing, building, maintaining, and updating the library’s web presence on LibGuides and assist with social media. Perform other library duties as assigned and needed, such as, administrative support and account management for SpringShare applications including LibGuides, LibAnswers, LibCal, and LibWizard and leadership for weeding and deaccession materials. Participate in answering all reference questions. Work with library staff in monitoring online chat. Rotate as needed at the reference desk and evening services. Student Support: (40%) Partner and collaborate with accessibility services, academic services, career services, and library services to develop and strengthen working relationships to promote a synchronized focus on all aspects of undergraduate, graduate, and online student success. Serves as the liaison in connecting and promoting library resources throughout ILS. Collaborate with student success involvement with orientation by coordinating opportunities for ILS areas to share services and resources. Apply data, assessment, and user feedback to evaluate effectiveness of student success, including career services, accessibility, and academic advising. Engage in professional learning and research related to best practices for ILS to support undergraduate, graduate, and online students’ awareness of all services. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning, & Student Success to evaluate the overall impact of an integrated division in providing services and support for all students. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning, & Student Success in sharing the ILS model in higher education. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning, & Student Success to identify funding resources to expand ILS. Participate in community life and activities of Columbia College, including student events. Support student success in designing, building, and maintaining a web presence on social media. Perform other duties as required to support career services, accessibility services, and academic advising. Additional Responsibilities: (10%) Assist with supporting and creating online activities when applicable. Serve on campus committees and task forces as assigned. Engage in learning more about student support services. Engage in professional development through various means, such as: Attending and presenting at local, state and/or regional conferences, workshops, webinars, etc. as budget permits. Staying current on professional literature and webinars. Conducting research for program development and presenting results. Other (5%): Assist with Accessibility Services as needed, such as helping with testing students. Performs other duties as assigned, within the general scope of the position description. Participates in community life and activities of Columbia College. Performs other related duties as assigned. MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS TO PERFORM WORK: Master’s Degree from an American Library Association (ALA) accredited master’s program (MLS/MLIS). Minimum of one year of overseeing and providing instruction on using electronic resources in academic libraries. Minimum of 1-2 years of professional experience in an academic library. Knowledge of current practices and pedagogy for electronic resources delivery and management. Knowledge of or interest in current practices and emerging trends in student success, including outreach, student engagement, and belonging. Ability to develop collegial relationships with colleagues, administrators, staff, faculty, and students across campus. Enthusiasm for learning how to provide integrated support services for all students at Columbia College. Enthusiasm for the learning culture of liberal arts institutions and for the mission of Columbia College. Demonstrated experience providing collaborative leadership. Demonstrated experience using best pedagogical practices for active learning preferred. Evidence of effective organizational skills and attention to detail. Excellent interpersonal, oral, written, collaboration, and presentation skills for communicating in multiple formats with a wide range of constituencies required. Competence with business math and written and verbal communication suitable in a business or professional setting. Skill in the use of Microsoft Office Suite, utilizing Outlook, Word, Excel and Microsoft Teams, or related software. MLIS/MLS is required for this position. All required documents must be completed for the application to be reviewed. Columbia College is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Ability to learn innovative technology. Ability to work independently with little direction. Ability to plan, organize, and prioritize work. Ability to meet schedules and timelines. Ability to understand and follow oral and written directions. Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Requires sedentary work that involves walking, standing, lifting, carrying, pushing, and pulling some of the time and involves exerting up to 10 pounds of force on a recurring basis or routine keyboard operations. WORKING CONDITIONS: Work environment risks exposure to no known environmental hazards. Applications To apply, a candidate must submit the following materials through the online submission portal (all in one pdf file except for the cover letter, which may be uploaded as a separate document): A cover letter describing teaching interests/experiences. An up-to-date curriculum vitae A teaching statement that includes teaching philosophy and methodology A statement regarding research and other scholarly endeavors Names and contact information for three references, and Copies of graduate transcripts. Unofficial copies of transcripts will suffice for the initial application stage. Review of completed applications will begin July 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success, Dr. Monika Rhue, at mrhue@columbiasc.edu. About Columbia College Columbia College was founded in 1854 as Columbia Female College by the Methodist Conference of South Carolina. Today, Columbia College offers a personalized liberal arts education that is open and inclusive to all. The College provides educational opportunities that develop students’ capacity for critical thought and expression, lifelong learning, and acceptance of personal responsibility. Columbia College empowers students to change the world with Courage for Innovation, Commitment to Social Justice, Confidence through Service, and Competence in Leadership. In furtherance of its mission, the College is responsive to the needs of students, the communities to which it belongs, and the greater global society. Columbia College encompasses the historic undergraduate day college as well as evening, online, and graduate programs. Total enrollment is approximately 1850. The College is noted for faculty who foster academic excellence through collaborative teaching and learning; a vital learning community in which character, ethics, and values are cultivated; the study of leadership enhanced by opportunities for practical application; programs that capitalize on advances in technology; and learning environments that extend beyond the classroom. EOE M/F/D/V Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/330359": {
      "title": "Research and Instruction Librarian",
      "description": "Research and Instruction Librarian You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Research and Instruction Librarian Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description JOB SUMMARY The Division of Integrated Learning Services at Columbia College invites applications for an Assistant Librarian in Research and Instruction position beginning September 2026 or earliest available date. The Research and Instruction Librarian promote and supports learning with a particular focus on the undergraduate programs at Columbia College, including first-year student programming and online support. The position provides leadership for developing the research and information literacy program for Columbia College. The Research and Instruction Librarian collaborate with faculty and staff to develop programming that cultivates a culture of implementing and using electronic and library resources among students, faculty, and staff. The Research and Instruction Librarian is responsible for evaluating and assessing Columbia College information literacy program, including developing learning outcomes that align with ACRL Information Literacy framework and the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC) library requirements . The position reports to the Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success while collaborating with other members of the Library and Learning Commons faculty and staff. This is a full-time (i.e., twelve-month), faculty position. Participation in evening and weekend shifts is required, as needed. This position is a twelve-month full-time faculty appointment. The candidate must demonstrate the ability to design and deliver instruction in a variety of formats and is expected to provide instruction and support to all undergraduate programs, including targeted support of first-year students in courses such as English 101 and 102, LA 100 and 110, etc. and some online courses . The candidate will incorporate the use of electronic resources in instruction. The candidate will work with the Educational Technology Librarian and campus’ instructional designer and advisors in supporting interactive and professional instructions to all students and faculty. The candidate is expected to have flexibility scheduling to meet the needs of the College. Faculty members are expected to participate in faculty governance and academic program activities, and to engage in service as well as scholarship and professional development. Columbia College is a liberal arts college that is committed to creating a learning community that values diversity and inclusion. We seek candidates who will be innovative in teaching, leadership, and research while building collaborative relationships in the college and profession. Columbia College offers a competitive benefits package. Qualifications ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Research and Information Literacy Management (60%) Develop, monitor, and maintain a comprehensive plan for information literacy (imagined broadly, to include media and digital literacy and misinformation/disinformation/bias/etc.) with learning outcomes and assessment in alignment with the ACRL framework for information literacy. Collaborate with faculty and staff to develop activities and assignments that involve students in inquiry that supports the mission of the college, information literacy, media and digital literacy, and ethical use of information, with commensurate support for in-person and online learners. Provide course-based information literacy support through instructional sessions, scaffolded involvement, embedded support, or otherwise. Provide instructions on how to use electronic resources and library materials for research. Provide leadership for collaborative management and development of SpringShare LibGuides and other resources. Develop workshops, skillshops, drop-in sessions, or other in-person and virtual events that engage students and faculty broadly in research and information literacy, including designing workshops and instructional materials that support students’ critical, ethical, and effective use of AI tools in academic contexts. Collaborate with other Learning Commons faculty and staff to support robust training for student employees to provide peer-to-peer mentoring in research and information literacy. Provide leadership for and participate in the effective delivery of research support in all modes, such as via chat, virtual appointments, etc. Participate in and support a consistent approach for documenting research and information literacy support and engagement in related programming for assessment purposes. Assume responsibility for the library and its programming, including supervising student assistants. Participate in the collection development of resources for the Reference and General Collections. Contribute to the development of LibGuides, and other online training modules and resources. Answer all research reference questions. Work with library staff in monitoring online chat. Rotate as needed at the reference desk and evening services. Assessment Management: (15%) Maintain all assessments for statistical reporting, such as ACRL, and Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC) with particular attention to assessing outcomes for undergraduate and graduate students understanding of information literacy, digital literacy, and AI literacy. Apply data, assessment, and user feedback to evaluate effectiveness of information literacy instruction, including library workshops, and trainings. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success to track, collect, and compile all library data for aligning with Columbia College’s mission, goals, and student success. Student Support: (15%) Partner and collaborate with accessibility services, academic services, career services, and library services to develop and strengthen working relationships to promote a synchronized focus on all aspects of research and instruction. Serves as the liaison in connecting and promoting research and instruction throughout ILS. Represent the library at orientation, career services, and other campus events promoting instruction, and library resources. Engage in professional learning and research related to best practices for ILS to support undergraduate, graduate, and online students’ awareness of all services. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success to evaluate the overall impact of an integrated division in providing services and support for all students. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success in sharing the ILS model in higher education. Work with the Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success to identify funding resources to expand ILS. Participate in community life and activities of Columbia College, including student events. Perform other duties as required to support library services, career services, accessibility services, and academic advising. Additional Responsibilities: (10%) Assist with supporting and creating online activities when applicable. Serve on campus committees and task forces as assigned. Engage in learning more about student support services. Engage in professional development through various means, such as: Attending and presenting at local, state and/or regional conferences, workshops, webinars, etc. as budget permits. Staying current on professional literature and webinars. Conducting research for program development and presenting results. Performs other duties as assigned, within the general scope of the position description. Participates in community life and activities of Columbia College. MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS TO PERFORM WORK: Master’s Degree from an American Library Association (ALA) accredited master’s program (MLS/MLIS). Minimum of one year in providing information literacy instruction in higher education. Minimum of 1-2 years of professional experience in an academic library. Knowledge of current practices and pedagogy for information literacy. Enthusiasm for learning how to provide integrated support services for all students at Columbia College. Enthusiasm for the learning culture of liberal arts institutions and for the mission of Columbia College. Demonstrated experience providing collaborative leadership. Demonstrated experience using best pedagogical practices for active learning preferred. Evidence of effective organizational skills and attention to detail. Competence with business math and written and verbal communication suitable in a business or professional setting. Skill in the use of Microsoft Office Suite, utilizing Outlook, Word, Excel and Microsoft Teams, or related software. MLIS/MLS is required for this position. All required documents must be completed for the application to be reviewed. Columbia College is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, AND ABILITIES: Ability to learn innovative technology. Ability to provide instruction to students, staff, and faculty. Ability to work independently with little direction. Ability to plan, organize, and prioritize work. Ability to meet schedules and timelines. Ability to understand and follow oral and written directions. Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with others. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Requires sedentary work that involves walking, standing, lifting, carrying, pushing, and pulling some of the time and involves exerting up to 10 pounds of force on a recurring basis or routine keyboard operations. WORKING CONDITIONS: Work environment risks exposure to no known environmental hazards. Applications To apply, a candidate must submit the following materials through the online submission portal (all in one pdf file except for the cover letter, which may be uploaded as a separate document): A cover letter describing teaching interests/experiences. An up-to-date curriculum vitae A teaching statement that includes teaching philosophy and methodology A statement regarding research and other scholarly endeavors Names and contact information for three references, and Copies of graduate transcripts. Unofficial copies of transcripts will suffice for the initial application stage. Review of completed applications will begin July 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries may be directed to Dean of Library, Learning & Student Success, Dr. Monika Rhue, at mrhue@columbiasc.edu. About Columbia College Columbia College was founded in 1854 as Columbia Female College by the Methodist Conference of South Carolina. Today, Columbia College offers a personalized liberal arts education that is open and inclusive to all. The College provides educational opportunities that develop students’ capacity for critical thought and expression, lifelong learning, and acceptance of personal responsibility. Columbia College empowers students to change the world with Courage for Innovation, Commitment to Social Justice, Confidence through Service, and Competence in Leadership. In furtherance of its mission, the College is responsive to the needs of students, the communities to which it belongs, and the greater global society. Columbia College encompasses the historic undergraduate day college as well as evening, online, and graduate programs. Total enrollment is approximately 1850. The College is noted for faculty who foster academic excellence through collaborative teaching and learning; a vital learning community in which character, ethics, and values are cultivated; the study of leadership enhanced by opportunities for practical application; programs that capitalize on advances in technology; and learning environments that extend beyond the classroom. EOE M/F/D/V Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
