    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/298414": {
      "title": "Online Academic Advisor",
      "description": "Online Academic Advisor You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Online Academic Advisor Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Position Type Full Time Apply Description Job Summary Provide academic advising, planning, and other support for online students in areas of course selection, registration procedures, degree requirements, and student success services in conjunction with the Registrar and Dean of Online Education. Proactively assist students with registration and academic questions and support students in problem solving regarding registration and related issues. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience Bachelor’s degree and two years’ experience in student personnel services or related field. Required Skills • Outstanding verbal and written communication skills with experience in higher education, advising, or customer service. • Ability to work independently with minimal supervision. • Ability to multi-task in a dynamic, fast-paced environment using multiple databases and software. • Outstanding organizational skills. • High level of proficiency in Microsoft Office. Experience with Jenzabar, , Canvas, and/or Slate a plus. • Strong attention to detail and follow through. Primary Responsibilities and Essential Job Functions • Maintain working knowledge of college curriculum, policies, and college resources. • Utilize CRM (Slate) to effectively manage new student transition to enrollment. • Complete Transfer Evaluation Forms for new students within 48 hours of receipt of transcripts. • Provide active, timely, and supportive academic advising to and communication with students, including follow through to non-responsive and non-registered applicants and returning students • Record all advising notes in the appropriate systems. • Track student progress towards completion and proactively assist with student retention from term to term. • Provide outstanding customer service to current and potential students over the phone, in email correspondence and in person. • Respond promptly to student issues and guide/direct prospective and current students to appropriate offices or information sources at Columbia College. • Maintain and comply with Columbia College policies and procedures to support academic standards. • Maintain relationships with appropriate campus constituents. • Other duties as assigned. • Ability to work evening and weekend hours during peak advising periods. • Extended or non-standard hours are possible at start or after training. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/274749": {
      "title": "Adjunct - Arts & Sciences",
      "description": "Adjunct - Arts & Sciences You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Adjunct - Arts & Sciences Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Position Type Part Time Apply Description Job Summary The School of Arts & Sciences at Columbia College seeks applications for adjunct faculty who can teach general education in-seat and online classes in the areas of Math, Communication, American Sign Language, Spanish, French, History, Philosophy, Religion, Sociology, Psychology, Art, Dance, English (First-Year Writing), English (General Education Literature), Music, Theater, Political Science, Criminal Justice, Graphic Design, Social Work, and Culture and Media Studies. Qualifications Minimum Requirements Master’s degree in any field including or in addition to 18 hours of graduate credits earned in the discipline in which the course(s) will be taught. A master’s degree in the field of study is highly preferred. Priority will be given to applicants with prior college teaching experience and familiarity with an LMS such as Canvas. Instructors are expected to adhere to all college policies and procedures, teach in accordance with established course goals and student learning outcomes, and uphold the mission and values of Columbia College. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Ability to plan, organize and prioritize work. Ability to meet schedules and timelines. Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing; excellent interpersonal skills. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Requires sedentary work that involves walking, standing, lifting, carrying, pushing and pulling some of the time and requires the ability to lift up to 50 pounds and the ability to exert up to 10 pounds of force on a recurring basis or routine keyboard operations. The successful candidate must arrange for all official undergraduate and graduate transcripts to be sent to the Provost’s Office at provost@columbiasc.edu . Questions about teaching in a particular academic area should be sent to the school dean, David McCracken, at dmccracken@columbiasc.edu or to the administrative assistant, Sue Hill, at shill@columbiasc.edu . About Columbia College Columbia College was founded in 1854 as Columbia Female College by the Methodist Conference of South Carolina. Today, Columbia College offers a personalized liberal arts education that is open and inclusive to all. The College provides educational opportunities that develop each student’s capacity for critical thought and expression, lifelong learning and inquiry, and acceptance of personal accountability and responsibility. Columbia College empowers students to change the world with Courage for Innovation, Commitment to Social Justice, Confidence through Service, and Competence in Leadership. In furtherance of its mission, the College is responsive to the needs of students, the communities to which it belongs, and the greater global society. Columbia College encompasses the historic undergraduate day college as well as evening, online, and graduate programs. Total enrollment is approximately 1800. The College is noted for faculty who foster academic excellence through collaborative teaching and learning; a vital learning community in which character, ethics, and values are cultivated; the study of leadership enhanced by opportunities for practical application; programs that capitalize on advances in technology; and learning environments that extend beyond the classroom. Columbia College is an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer. For information about the College, visit www.columbiasc.edu . For information about the city of Columbia, South Carolina, please visit www.columbiasc.net . Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
    "https://www.paycomonline.net/v4/ats/web.php/portal/716F3DBB868513EDA5A7657F756BBDD8/jobs/271415": {
      "title": "Development Manager",
      "description": "Development Manager You need to enable JavaScript to run this app. Careers Sign In Create Account Development Manager Columbia College - Columbia, SC 29203 Apply Overview Salary Range $60,000.00 - $70,000.00 Salary Position Type Full Time Apply Description JOB SUMMARY The Development Manager for Corporate & Foundation Relations is responsible for securing philanthropic support from corporations, foundations, and other institutional partners to advance Columbia College’s mission, programs, scholarships, and strategic priorities. The role manages a portfolio of funders, develops proposals, stewards donors, and collaborates across campus. Qualifications ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Corporate & Foundation Fundraising Identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward corporate and foundation prospects. Build and maintain relationships with key donors, alumni, and volunteers to identify prospects, gather intelligence, encourage ongoing support and engagement. Develop proposals and grant applications supporting institutional priorities. Support sponsorship solicitations for sporting events and community festivals. Manage a portfolio of institutional funders. Strategic Planning & Research Conduct research to identify new funding opportunities. Track philanthropic trends to inform strategy. Collaborate with faculty and administrators to align funding with priorities. Proposal Development & Reporting Create compelling proposals and grant reports. Collaborate with marketing and communications to create compelling partnership collateral. Maintain calendars for deadlines and deliverables. Ensure donor reporting reflects measurable impact. Donor Stewardship & Partnership Management Implement stewardship plans. Coordinate site visits, recognition, and engagement. Utilize CRM to track prospect and donor interactions accurately. Establishes and tracks performance indicators and adjusts strategies as needed to meet fundraising goals. Works with Director of Advancement Services to effectively track campaigns, proposals, appeals and contributions within our CRM - Raiser’s Edge NXT. Support the Director of Annual Giving & Stewardship in the production and management of stewardship activities, communications, and giving societies. Collaboration & Campus Engagement Partner with faculty and staff to develop fundable projects and proposals. Work with career services, student recruitment. athletics and other units within the College to strengthen corporate partnerships throughout the College. Support events that highlight corporate and foundation philanthropy. Serve as liaison to community partnership projects across campus (when appropriate) MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS TO PERFORM WORK: Bachelor’s degree required; advanced degree preferred. 3+ years fundraising or grant writing experience. Strong writing and relationship-building skills. Ability to manage multiple deadlines. Columbia College is an equal opportunity employer. All applicants will be considered for employment without attention to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, veteran or disability status. Share job details to Terms of Use | Privacy Policy | © 2026 Paycom | All Rights Reserved. X This website uses cookies to customize and improve your experience. If you are a California resident, you may be entitled to certain rights regarding your personal information. Additional information about our data collection practices and location specific notices is available on our privacy policy. Accept Cookies"
    },
